---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import markHiking from '../../images/markhikebig.webp';
---

<BaseLayout title="Leadership profile — Mark Gingrass" description="GS-14 program leader, COR III, and Air Force veteran with 20+ years leading federal digital transformation, mission systems, and technology delivery.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Profile / digital transformation leadership</p>
    <h1>I lead where the decisions are hard.</h1>
    <p class="lede">I set direction, connect mission priorities to technical decisions, and hold delivery accountable.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="section compact">
    <div class="shell profile-grid">
      <div class="profile-sticky">
        <Image class="profile-image" src={markHiking} alt="Mark Gingrass outdoors on a hiking trail" widths={[560, 900]} sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 42vw" loading="eager" />
      </div>
      <div class="profile-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">The short version</p>
        <h2>I work at the strategy level—and stay close to reality.</h2>
        <p>I’m a GS-14 program manager and COR III at FDA, leading research-system modernization that serves more than 400 regulatory scientists. My work spans product direction, solutions architecture, acquisition, data integration, and responsible AI inside the agency boundary.</p>
        <p>I also create and ship my own products. For TutorialRepo and PetShots, I set the product direction, shape the architecture and experience, and manage the path to production. That work keeps my technical judgment current and makes me better at leading teams and vendors.</p>
        <p>Government systems rarely fail for one reason. The mission gets watered down in the requirement. The contract rewards the wrong thing. The architecture ignores the operating environment. I’ve spent more than 20 years finding and fixing those gaps.</p>
        <p>I’m ready for a broader mandate in digital transformation, technical program leadership, or solutions architecture. If you need someone who can set direction across an organization and still challenge the technical details, that’s where I fit.</p>

        <div class="profile-stack">
          <div class="profile-item"><span>Mission context</span><p>Federal and defense experience from enlisted Air Force service through GS-14 leadership, including acquisition, security review, governance, and long-lived programs.</p></div>
          <div class="profile-item"><span>Program scale</span><p>Product strategy and modernization leadership for systems serving 400+ regulatory scientists.</p></div>
          <div class="profile-item"><span>Accountability</span><p>COR III experience overseeing contractor performance and translating program outcomes into executable delivery.</p></div>
          <div class="profile-item"><span>Technical judgment</span><p>Enough depth across federal research systems, information retrieval, native iOS, serverless AWS, infrastructure as code, and applied AI to test assumptions and make sound decisions.</p></div>
          <div class="profile-item"><span>Education</span><p>Master of Business Administration and Bachelor’s in Computer Science, earned while working full-time.</p></div>
          <div class="profile-item"><span>Technical education</span><p>Taught C++ to adult learners at Metro Technology Centers and built technical courses for YouTube, Udemy, and TutorialRepo. Clear explanation is part of the architecture role, not a side skill.</p></div>
        </div>

        <div class="button-row" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
          <a class="button primary" href="mailto:mark@markgingrass.com">Start a conversation ↗</a>
          <a class="button" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/markgingrass/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section paper">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionHeading index="01" label="Operating principles" title="How I work." />
      <div class="role-grid" style="border-color: rgba(11,13,12,.18);">
        <article class="role-card"><h3>Make the decision visible</h3><p style="color:#555e57">Architecture and program choices should expose the tradeoff, the owner, and what would cause us to revisit them.</p></article>
        <article class="role-card"><h3>Ship the useful core</h3><p style="color:#555e57">A smaller release in users’ hands creates better evidence than a perfect roadmap still waiting for consensus.</p></article>
        <article class="role-card"><h3>Build for whoever comes next</h3><p style="color:#555e57">Federal programs outlast their leaders. Systems and decisions need to be understandable, operable, and defensible after the room changes.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section compact">
    <div class="shell case-grid">
      <div class="case-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">Away from the whiteboard</p>
        <h3>Outside, preferably with some elevation.</h3>
        <p>I hike, camp, mountain bike, run, skateboard, and make an annual trip to West Virginia for Class V whitewater. Ollie, my Golden Retriever, handles trail operations whenever conditions permit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-steps">
        <div class="case-step"><span>01</span><div><h4>Air Force veteran</h4><p>A foundation in service, discipline, and mission accountability.</p></div></div>
        <div class="case-step"><span>02</span><div><h4>Community volunteer</h4><p>Tree planting and trail cleanup in the places I use.</p></div></div>
        <div class="case-step"><span>03</span><div><h4>Product creator</h4><p>If a useful product does not exist, my instinct is to define it, direct it, and get it into the world.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
